1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,571
Coach: Even when you are listening carefully,

2
00:00:02,571 --> 00:00:06,857
Coach: you may respond to the wrong part of a sentence.

3
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:10,286
Coach: A correction gives you another chance to understand.

4
00:00:10,286 --> 00:00:16,714
Coach: The useful move is to update your understanding before defending the reply you already gave.

5
00:00:17,064 --> 00:00:21,778
Coach: We will use an authored example from the same workshop discussion.

6
00:00:21,778 --> 00:00:25,207
Coach: The group has thirty minutes and one facilitator.

7
00:00:25,207 --> 00:00:28,207
Coach: The following misunderstanding belongs to the example.

8
00:00:28,207 --> 00:00:32,064
Coach: It is not something we have heard you say.

9
00:00:32,414 --> 00:00:35,843
Noah: If several pairs lose their place at once,

10
00:00:35,843 --> 00:00:38,414
Noah: one facilitator may not reach everyone.

11
00:00:38,764 --> 00:00:42,193
You: Could we put more paper on each table?

12
00:00:42,543 --> 00:00:47,257
Noah: I mean help with the steps, not a shortage of paper.

13
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:52,321
Coach: The first reply treated the problem as a shortage of materials.

14
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:55,321
Coach: Noah has now made the issue clearer:

15
00:00:55,321 --> 00:01:00,464
Coach: several pairs might need help with the instructions at the same time.

16
00:01:00,464 --> 00:01:03,464
Coach: More paper does not address that concern.

17
00:01:03,814 --> 00:01:06,814
Coach: A brief acknowledgement can mark the update.

18
00:01:06,814 --> 00:01:09,385
Coach: Thanks for clarifying is one option.

19
00:01:09,385 --> 00:01:14,528
Coach: It is useful only if the next words reflect the corrected meaning.

20
00:01:14,528 --> 00:01:17,528
Coach: You do not need a long apology,

21
00:01:17,528 --> 00:01:23,528
Coach: and you do not need to pretend that you agree with every proposed solution.

22
00:01:23,878 --> 00:01:28,164
Coach: Before you rehearse, identify the concern in your own words.

23
00:01:28,164 --> 00:01:31,592
Coach: What could happen if several pairs need help,

24
00:01:31,592 --> 00:01:34,592
Coach: and why does having one facilitator matter?

25
00:01:34,592 --> 00:01:40,164
Coach: Keep that issue separate from the question of how much paper is available.

26
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:54,371
Coach: Now form a short English reply to Noah's correction.

27
00:01:54,371 --> 00:01:59,085
Coach: You could acknowledge the clarification, show which concern you now understand,

28
00:01:59,085 --> 00:02:02,942
Coach: and suggest or ask about one relevant next step.

29
00:02:02,942 --> 00:02:05,514
Coach: Use only the parts you need.

30
00:02:05,514 --> 00:02:09,371
Coach: This is private rehearsal: nothing is recorded or scored.

31
00:02:09,721 --> 00:02:16,578
Coach: In Watch mode, there is a short time to try before one possible reply is shown.

32
00:02:16,578 --> 00:02:23,435
Coach: In Practice mode, take as long as you need, then choose the model or skip it.

33
00:02:23,435 --> 00:02:29,007
Coach: Seeing the model does not mean that you have completed the speaking task.

34
00:03:04,357 --> 00:03:05,643
You: Thanks for clarifying.

35
00:03:05,643 --> 00:03:10,357
You: You mean several pairs may need help at the same time.

36
00:03:10,357 --> 00:03:16,786
You: Could we keep a step card visible, and ask which steps people need help with?

37
00:03:17,136 --> 00:03:18,850
Noah: A card might help.

38
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:21,850
Noah: What if someone still cannot follow it?

39
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:26,914
Coach: This model acknowledges the correction and addresses help with the steps.

40
00:03:26,914 --> 00:03:31,629
Coach: Noah still has a question about someone who needs more support.

41
00:03:31,629 --> 00:03:35,057
Coach: The reply has opened a more relevant discussion;

42
00:03:35,057 --> 00:03:40,200
Coach: it has not guaranteed that everyone will get the help they need.

43
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:43,979
Coach: Whether you tried a reply, watched the model,

44
00:03:43,979 --> 00:03:47,407
Coach: or skipped it, the same question is useful:

45
00:03:47,407 --> 00:03:51,693
Coach: did the response address the concern that was actually stated?

46
00:03:51,693 --> 00:03:54,693
Coach: You can check a reply you remember,

47
00:03:54,693 --> 00:03:58,550
Coach: or simply use the question for your next attempt.

48
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:02,329
Coach: Notice also whether the reply adds an assumption.

49
00:04:02,329 --> 00:04:08,329
Coach: Saying you are worried about not having enough materials would keep the original misunderstanding.

50
00:04:08,329 --> 00:04:15,186
Coach: Saying you want us to cancel the workshop would add a claim Noah did not make.

51
00:04:15,536 --> 00:04:19,393
Coach: You may still disagree about what to do next.

52
00:04:19,393 --> 00:04:24,107
Coach: Understanding the concern does not commit you to a particular plan.

53
00:04:24,107 --> 00:04:27,107
Coach: For now, keep the two questions separate:

54
00:04:27,107 --> 00:04:33,107
Coach: what does the person mean, and what response would help the group examine it?

55
00:04:41,457 --> 00:04:47,028
Coach: If you want another attempt, return to the rehearsal and change one thing:

56
00:04:47,028 --> 00:04:50,885
Coach: make the corrected concern clearer, remove an added assumption,

57
00:04:50,885 --> 00:04:53,885
Coach: or make the next question more specific.

58
00:04:53,885 --> 00:04:56,028
Coach: You choose what needs attention.

59
00:04:56,028 --> 00:05:00,314
Coach: The player cannot judge a reply it has not heard.
